Verdict: PASS. The fix does what #1015 asked, and I wrote the verdict to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

• **What changed:** when `-p` writes to a pipe, a SIGTERM now flushes the text yoyo was holding, prints the `(interrupted)` note on stderr and exits 143. That is the same thing Ctrl+C already did (Ctrl+C still exits 130). The Ctrl+C code was moved into one shared function, interrupt::finish_interrupted_turn, and both signal arms call it rather than each having a copy.
• **The risk the task flagged is handled:** registering a SIGTERM handler would normally replace "die on SIGTERM" for the whole process. Here the handler is only registered for piped single-shot runs. Once registered, it always ends the process with 143, even when the signal lands during a tool call. A run on a terminal, the REPL, --print, json or stream-json registers nothing and still dies the default way.
• **Tests:** I re-ran the three signal tests in tests/print_stdout_contract.rs and all passed:
  • SIGTERM puts exactly PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n on stdout, the note on stderr, and exits 143.
  • SIGTERM during a bash sleep 30 tool call exits 143 within 10 seconds instead of waiting out the sleep.
  • The existing SIGINT test is unchanged and still passes.
• **New code is all wired up:** every new function is called somewhere in the same change, and both exit-code sites in main.rs use the new mapping.
• **Docs:** docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md and the ARCHITECTURE.md entry are both updated.

I didn't redo the live probe the task asked for (timeout -s TERM 12 ./target/debug/yoyo -p "..." | wc -c) because it needs a real provider. The stub-based tests cover the same behaviour.
